{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1692361792/16/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"41ace6b5\" lines=\"7-26\">\n<description>\nNormalize vertical two-color bars between paired marker columns around the marker rows.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- A row of red marker pixels sits directly above a row of gray marker pixels\n  in the same alternating columns.\n- The columns between adjacent markers contain vertical bars made from a\n  contiguous teal run above a contiguous blue run.\n\nRule\n- Keep the marker pixels and all non-bar background cells fixed.\n- Let h be the maximum teal-run height among the bar columns; paint teal in\n  every bar column for h rows ending at the red marker row.\n- In each bar column, move its blue run to start at the gray marker row and\n  fill all lower cells in that column with maroon.\n\nKey insight\n- The marker rows are a baseline: teal is normalized into a common upper\n  band, while each blue run keeps its own height below the baseline.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"20fb2937\" lines=\"63-77\">\n<description>\nReplace each marker below the separator with the mapped top legend square centered on that marker.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background surrounds a top legend, one solid non-background separator row, and a lower marker field.\n- The legend contains equal-size filled square sprites, with a singleton marker below each sprite and aligned to its center column.\n- The lower field contains only background and marker colors from the legend.\n\nRule:\n- Pair legend markers to legend sprites by left-to-right position.\n- Discard the legend and separator; keep the grid width and the rows below the separator.\n- In the lower field, replace every marker pixel by a filled square of the paired sprite color, centered at the marker location.\n\nKey insight: The top area is a positional color legend, and the actual output is the post-separator marker field expanded by that legend.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"22208ba4\" lines=\"12-27\">\n<description>\nMove the repeated boundary color's rectangles one block inward while leaving singleton boundary rectangles in place.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the most common color.\n- Foreground objects are solid monochrome rectangles touching a grid corner.\n- Exactly one foreground color appears as multiple separate 4-connected objects; other colors appear once.\n\nRule:\n- Clear the multiple objects of the repeated color from their original positions.\n- For each cleared rectangle, translate it away from the row and column border it touches by exactly its height and width.\n- Keep singleton-color objects unchanged; overlapping translated rectangles of the same color merge.\n\nKey insight:\n- Multiplicity selects the moving color, and each boundary rectangle's touched edges determine its inward diagonal translation.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"291dc1e1\" lines=\"7-22\">\n<description>\nReorder and stack rectangles by proximity to two colored edge lines.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Two edge lines (h + v) meet at a corner marked by a unique color.\n- \"Blue\" line is color 1 if present, else the longer line; other is \"red.\"\n- Solid rectangles in the interior.\n\nRule:\n- Sort rectangles by (distance to blue edge, distance to red edge).\n- If the blue edge is vertical, rotate each rectangle 90° CCW.\n- Stack top-to-bottom, centered horizontally.\n\nKey insight: Edge-line colors encode a coordinate system; proximity to each axis determines stacking order and rotation.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"984d8a3e\" lines=\"5-21\">\n<description>\nShift each row's dominant middle-color run left until its right edge aligns with the leftmost original right edge, moving displaced left-side pixels after the run.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has three colors.\n- The most common color forms one contiguous horizontal run in every row.\n- Pixels before those runs share one side color, and pixels after those runs share the other side color.\n\nRule:\n- Find the minimum right edge column among the middle-color runs.\n- In each row, keep the run width and the trailing right-side color count.\n- Place the middle-color run so its right edge is at that minimum column, unless the run is too wide, in which case start it at column 0.\n- Fill any remaining non-trailing cells with the left-side color.\n\nKey insight:\nThe varying row prefixes are movable padding; the stable feature is the leftmost right boundary of the middle-color shape.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"3d588dc9\" lines=\"95-109\">\n<description>\nReplace the active black cutout's tail with background and mark the cutout side facing a colored source region.\n\nInput structure:\n- The dominant color is background, with black rectangle-plus-tail cutouts, one gray source region, and colored distractors.\n- Non-gray colored regions and single pixels are distractors/blockers, even when they are multi-pixel staircase shapes.\n\nRule:\n- Find each black cutout whose largest solid rectangle leaves exactly a two-pixel tail.\n- The active gray source region is visible only by horizontal rays through background from a cutout's left or right side.\n- Select the unique cutout side with the most same-source visible rows, erase that cutout's tail to background, and recolor the full selected vertical side pink (6).\n\nKey insight:\n- The colored regions do not move; their horizontal line-of-sight identifies which rectangular cutout is being opened and which side receives the pink mark.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"bd5af378\" lines=\"25-42\">\n<description>\nRewrite a corner L block into two nested L borders and a diagonal trace.\n\nInput structure\nA rectangular grid has exactly two colors: one color forms a one-cell-thick\nL on two adjacent outer edges, and the other color fills the remaining\nrectangle.\n\nRule\nNormalize the L to the top-left corner. Paint the outer top-left L with the\nfill color, the next inner L with the original border color, and the main\ndiagonal of the remaining inner rectangle with the fill color. All other\ncells become color 8, then the original orientation is restored.\n\nKey insight\nThe corner of the input L determines the orientation; after flipping to a\ncanonical top-left L, the output is the same nested-layer pattern for every\nexample.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"78332cb0\" lines=\"10-23\">\n<description>\nReorder 5×5 blocks into a connected strip by matching edge foreground pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background; sep = divider color (full row/column lines).\n- Several 5×5 sprite blocks in a grid, separated by sep lines.\n\nRule:\n- Two blocks \"connect\" when their facing edges share foreground pixels at the same offset (e.g., A's right edge ∩ B's left edge).\n- Chain all blocks in the orientation (horizontal or vertical) that admits a complete sequence.\n- Output is a 1×N strip of blocks in chain order, separated by sep lines.\n\nKey insight: Edge-contact overlap determines both block ordering and output orientation.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"45a5af55\" lines=\"5-18\">\n<description>\nBuild nested square frames from a stack of horizontal color stripes.\n\nInput structure:\n- Full-width horizontal stripes of distinct non-bg colors, stacked vertically.\n- Each stripe has uniform color and variable height (1-4 rows).\n\nRule:\n- Read stripes bottom to top.\n- Bottom stripe becomes the center solid square with side length = stripe height.\n- Each subsequent stripe wraps the current shape with a square frame of thickness = stripe height, in that stripe's color.\n\nKey insight: Stripe height doubles as both frame thickness and (for the innermost) center-square side length; output dimensions are fully determined by the stripe heights.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"c920a713\" lines=\"87-102\">\n<description>\nUntangle overlapping colored rectangular frames into concentric square frames ordered by their visible crossings.\n\nInput structure\n- The background contains several one-cell-thick axis-aligned rectangular frames, one color per frame.\n- Frames overlap, and the visible color at an overlap records a directed crossing from the hidden frame to the visible frame.\n- Each foreground color still has visible pixels on the perimeter of its full rectangular bounding box.\n\nRule\n- Infer each color's full rectangular frame from the bounding box of its visible pixels.\n- Add a directed edge A -> B when B is visible at a crossing of A and B's frame perimeters.\n- Find the unique path that visits every frame color once, then render those colors as one-pixel-thick concentric square frames from outside to inside.\n\nKey insight\n- The output is not a crop of the input; the crossing graph gives a single frame order, and that order is redrawn as nested rings.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"c803e39c\" lines=\"31-40\">\n<description>\nExpand the second mask into a block grid by stamping the first mask with the two payload colors.\n\nInput structure\n- A background grid has four panels separated by three full-height separator columns.\n- The first two panels contain same-size binary masks; the last two contain solid same-size color squares.\nRule\n- Use the second mask as an outer layout. For each foreground cell in that outer mask, place a tile equal to the first mask, coloring its foreground with the first solid-square color and its background with the second solid-square color; outer background cells become all second solid-square color.\nKey insight\n- The output is the Kronecker product of the two binary masks, with the two right-hand panel colors providing the palette.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"2de01db2\" lines=\"28-41\">\n<description>\nInvert each row's native foreground color against the black background.\n\nInput structure\n- A black-background grid whose rows each have one native foreground color plus optional distractor colors.\n- The native row color is the most frequent non-bg color in that row; if tied, its run starts leftmost among tied colors.\n\nRule\n- For each row independently, turn pixels of the native row color into bg.\n- Turn every other pixel in that row, including bg and distractor colors, into the native row color.\n\nKey insight\n- Distractor colors are not preserved objects; they mark cells that participate in the binary complement of the row's native color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"c9680e90\" lines=\"52-67\">\n<description>\nMove each mirrored bottom source pixel to the far end of its trail and redraw the top half as its reflection.\n\nInput structure\n- A solid non-background row separates equal-height top and bottom halves.\n- The top half has one marker color on the background.\n- In the bottom half, exactly one color has positions that mirror the top markers across the separator, and the other color forms trails touching those source pixels.\n\nRule\n- Identify the bottom source color by reflecting each bottom color into the top half and matching the top marker set.\n- For every bottom source pixel, follow its unique 4-connected trail of the other bottom color to the trail endpoint.\n- Clear the old top markers, bottom sources, and trails; place the source color at each endpoint and place the top marker color at the reflected endpoint.\n\nKey insight\n- The top half is only a mirror display of the bottom moving sources; the actual motion is encoded by the trail attached to each bottom source pixel.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"14\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"8698868d\" lines=\"9-23\">\n<description>\nMatch sprites to frames by connected-hole count, then nest each sprite inside its frame.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = azure (8). Large hollow squares (frames) arranged in a grid; smaller filled squares (sprites) placed elsewhere.\n- Each frame and sprite has a distinct color. Sprites may contain holes (non-sprite-color pixels).\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
